ess we  make the infrastructure investments  to support high-speed rail and and as  you think about how the demographic of  our country is changing from what it was  a hundred years ago to people moving to  urban areas and very concentrated urban  corridors number one and number two the  growth of ride sharing where people  don't own cars and they use ride sharing  more and more  mass transit and commuter trains should  play a more important role in how we  live in urban areas so what do you  consider your imperative first  imperative is to get the infrastructure  fixed  and the work we did at penn station  shows amtrak can do a great job doing  this and we have a lot of investment to  make in the northeast corridor second  we've got to clean up our trains run our  trains on time  fix the interiors of our trains  and grow our services in the regions  that provide the highest levels of  service to the communities around the  country what kind of window are you  looking at i mean how monumental is that  you took delta from a small southern  carrier to what many feel is the is the  best u.s airline the biggest u.s airline  amtrak's in uh  a lot of trouble uh penn station's been  in a lot of trouble how how long does  that take if you think you can turn it  around how long does it take to do that  well it's a multi-year effort and part  of the planning process  with our board is to lay out what those  plans are a long-term fleet plan a  long-term engineering plan and of course  the gateway project here in new york 10  years 20 years  well it took 10 years at delta so it'll  it'll be a multi-year project let me put  it that way and making the switch from  planes to trains was it a learning curve  for you did you look in the you had no  real experience did you look in the  mirror and say i can do this or were you  a little concerned well you're always  concerned when you don't know the  subject matter so it's exactly it's a  lot of homework idea how do you move  people in one place to another yeah it's  capital intensive it's a network  business safety is really important  customer service is important very  similar on the other hand it's very  different technology but i'm fortunate  i have a co-ceo  who also came out of retirement on a  volunteer basis wick mormon how'd they  convince you guys to do it  huh  well i i wanted to serve in some  capacity okay  so tell me about this the terms of the  future is there technology happening uh  that might be uh dramatically changed  the way transportation works in  america well if you think about the  technology  well i mean the the thing we could do is  if we could just get our train speeds up  and operate more non-stop service in  very  densely populated urban corridors it  would be a great service to to the  traveling public in america think about  what asella has done  and we're on the verge of putting a  newest newest seller system in place  that what is the new seller well we're  right now we're under construction on on  replacing the current asella fleet with  uh 28 new acela train sets that are  going to increase the capacity about 40  percent  and allow us to do um half hourly  service from uh washington dc to new  york and new york to boston on the hour  there was also talk that they're going  to make the the space between the seats  shorter could you please make sure they  don't do that well we're not going to  make the space between the seats shorter  that was  an idea you heard that rumor well we  talked about doing it because one of the  questions we've had is making it more  affordable i mean remember the thing  about  much of what we do at amtrak our average  fare is about eighty dollars  and on the commuter rails it's even less  than ten dollars on average  but the bottom line is we want to make  it affordable and usable for people and  one of our great advantages is there's  no middle seats there's tons of leg room  our coach on amtrak  is much much better than first class on  an airline so you're not going to change  the seat spacing no okay should you  privatize it  well the question is i mean you can't  really privatize it because  there are pieces of it that have to be  subsidized i mean you could privatize if  you wanted the pieces that are  profitable but that wouldn't make a lot  of sense i mean look this is basic  infrastructure  we subsidized  [Music]  i think the subsidy last year for  highways was about 45 billion the  subsidy for aviation was about 16  billion and when you think about what we  do and what's sort of fundamental to  public policy it's to fund  infrastructure all right richard  anderson so far so good the summer of  hell did not happen here in new york we  actually did we did a great job and  thanks to our partners at long island  railroad and new jersey transit thank  you sir

